A deathtrap yes, but one you basically need to leap into
voluntarily.  Consider that he put on the amulet of unchanging
BEFORE using the wizmode #polyself.  Normally, if you have the amulet
on you won't be able to polymorph into the jelly to start with, and
once you polymorph, you won't get any turns in which to put the amulet
on.  So you eventually do get knocked out of it.

After experimenting in explore mode, I was able to find a way to do it
in a normal game.  If you put on =polycontrol, quaff a potion of
speed, THEN polymorph somehow, the speed from the potion will override
the speed 0 from the monster until the potion runs out.  You still
can't move, but you can take other actions, like putting on the
amulet.  If you then wait for the potion to wear off, you won't be
able to move for the rest of the game, and will eventually starve.

On the positive side, by the time I starved, I had gained four levels
and multiplied into 11 tame blue jellies named Test, all while frozen
in place.  At one point, I also seemed to go from fainting to merely
hungry, but I didn't catch how it happened.  But since without the
amulet of unchanging you do turn back eventually, this is actually
a potentially useful tactic.  In wizard mode, I put on a ring of
poly control, created a roomful of soldier ants, and zapped myself
with the wand of polymorph.  Three charges and a few hundred turns
later, I was level 7 and the ants were all dead.  A tactic that's
not terribly likely to succeed, but if an early game wizard with
the right starting equipment gets into trouble...

As an aside, the RNG was unusually generous with starting equipment
for all these games.  The first one gave me a /poly (0:8), a =SD,
and a magic marker (0:30). The second gave a potion of holy water,
a scroll of charging, and a marker (0:75), and the third gave
a ?charging and a =regen.  And the wizmode game had another /poly.
No doubt the next REAL game will yield two scrolls of punishment, a
wand of opening and a ring of con + 1.



